WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.045
A measure assigns nonnegative size to allowed sets and assigns zero to the empty set

00:00:07.045 --> 00:00:12.804
The allowed sets form a sigma algebra, closed under complements and countable unions

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:17.233
This specifies which events or regions can be measured consistently.

00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:21.215
For disjoint measurable sets, sizes add

00:00:21.215 --> 00:00:30.156
With four equally likely outcomes, a two-outcome event has probability one half and its two-outcome complement has probability one half

00:00:30.156 --> 00:00:32.391
Their union has probability one

00:00:32.391 --> 00:00:35.967
Probability is a measure with total size one.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.294
Different measures describe different notions of size

00:00:40.294 --> 00:00:44.185
Lebesgue measure on the line agrees with interval length

00:00:44.185 --> 00:00:48.942
A singleton has length zero even though it is not empty

00:00:48.942 --> 00:00:52.833
Counting measure, by contrast, assigns that singleton size one.

00:00:54.133 --> 00:01:00.559
Countable additivity implies that a countable union of zero-length singletons still has measure zero

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:06.067
The rational numbers therefore have Lebesgue measure zero, although they are dense

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:12.033
The few rational points drawn are only an illustration of that countable-set argument.
